			"90": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 7: NORMAL(GAUSSIAN) DISTRIBUTION\n\nUntil this example we've been working in uniform distribution.\nThis means that every possible outcome of the Random node has an equal chance to happen.\nThis is similar to rolling a six-sided dice, the chances of a given side being rolled is 16.66% for every side.\nBut, sometimes you want to cheat the outcome a bit, this is where normal distribution (also known as gaussian distribution) comes into play.\n\nInstead of a min and max we have a mean and deviation.\nThe easiest way to think of the mean is as a starting point or \"expected value\".\nThe deviation is how much we are going to deviate from the mean.\nThis results in a distribution of values known as a bell curve.\nMost of the results will be near the mean, with the higher deviations being the most unlikely to happen.\n\nDon't worry if you don't understand the patch below, but take a look at the output of the patch.\nWe are distributing 100 red dots using normal distribution with a mean 0 and deviation of 0.7.\nAs you can see, most of the dots are distributed in the center.\nThere are some strays out the outer sides, but they are rare."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 857.5869140625,
					"width": 1746.615966796875,
					"x": 184.712646484375,
					"y": 2714.904296875
				},
